rows.body:
The scrape status code `438` is a hardcoded magic number with no documentation explaining what it represents. A reader has no way to know that `438` is Plaid's reconnect-required error, or why `532` was removed, without external context from the PR description.

Add a brief comment documenting the meaning of the status code so future maintainers understand it:

```ts
// 438 is Plaid's reconnect-required status indicating the card needs re-authentication
const reauthScrapeStatuses: number[] = [438];
```

The bare literal `5` in `foundSuggestionsCount < 5` is a magic number. It silently encodes the "minimum number of already-found room suggestions below which we still hit the server" threshold, which is not self-explanatory to a reader. This line is being rewritten as part of the new `useCallback`, so the threshold should be extracted into a documented named constant.

Define a named constant (ideally alongside the other autocomplete limits in `CONST.AUTO_COMPLETE_SUGGESTER`) and reference it here:

```tsx
// in CONST.ts, under AUTO_COMPLETE_SUGGESTER
MIN_ROOM_SUGGESTIONS_BEFORE_SERVER_SEARCH: 5,

// in SuggestionMention.tsx
if (prefixType === '#' && foundSuggestionsCount < CONST.AUTO_COMPLETE_SUGGESTER.MIN_ROOM_SUGGESTIONS_BEFORE_SERVER_SEARCH && isGroupPolicyReport) {
    searchInServer(searchPrefix, policyID);
}
```

The modal action is compared against the raw string literal `'CONFIRM'`. This is a magic string: the value is already defined as a named constant `ModalActions.CONFIRM` (exported from `@components/Modal/Global/ModalContext`), and `showConfirmModal` resolves with `{action: 'CONFIRM' | 'CLOSE'}`. Using the literal risks silent breakage if the constant ever changes and loses the type-level guarantee.

Import and use the constant instead:

```tsx
import {ModalActions} from '@components/Modal/Global/ModalContext';

// ...
if (result.action === ModalActions.CONFIRM) {
    Navigation.navigate(
        ROUTES.SETTINGS_WALLET_ENABLE_GLOBAL_REIMBURSEMENTS_BUSINESS.getRoute(bankAccountID, CONST.ENABLE_GLOBAL_REIMBURSEMENTS.PAGE_NAME.BUSINESS_INFO.REGISTRATION_NUMBER),
    );
}
```

The `setTimeout(..., 200)` delay is a magic number with no named constant or documentation of what `200` (ms) represents. The same unexplained `200` already appears elsewhere in this file, compounding the ambiguity.

Extract it to a named constant that conveys intent, e.g.:

```tsx
const MODAL_DISMISS_DELAY_MS = 200;
// ...
setTimeout(() => {
    item.onPress();
    delete onModalHide.current;
}, MODAL_DISMISS_DELAY_MS);
```

The `0x20` threshold is a magic value with no documentation or named constant. It represents the boundary below which characters are ASCII control characters (like the `\n` line separators callers split on) that legitimately have no glyph and must be skipped. An earlier revision of this PR carried a comment explaining exactly this, but it was removed, leaving a bare hex literal whose intent is not self-evident to the next reader.

Extract it into a named constant or restore an explanatory comment, for example:

```ts
// Skip ASCII control characters (e.g. the `\n` line separators callers split on) that legitimately have no glyph.
const FIRST_PRINTABLE_CODE_POINT = 0x20;

function canTypefaceRenderText(typeface: SkTypeface, text: string): boolean {
    for (const char of text) {
        const codePoint = char.codePointAt(0);
        if (codePoint === undefined || codePoint < FIRST_PRINTABLE_CODE_POINT) {
            continue;
        }
        // ...
    }
}
```

The illustration dimensions `160` and `140` are hardcoded magic numbers with no explanation of what they represent. Previously these values lived in the named style `commuterExclusionStaticIllustration`, which self-documented their purpose; inlining them as bare literals removes that context and makes future changes harder to reason about.

Extract them into named constants (or a `CONST` entry) so their meaning is clear, e.g.:

```ts
const COMMUTER_EXCLUSION_ILLUSTRATION_WIDTH = 160;
const COMMUTER_EXCLUSION_ILLUSTRATION_HEIGHT = 140;
// ...
imageWidth: COMMUTER_EXCLUSION_ILLUSTRATION_WIDTH,
imageHeight: COMMUTER_EXCLUSION_ILLUSTRATION_HEIGHT,
```

The upper bound `10` in `number >= 1 && number <= 10` is a magic number. It silently encodes the fact that written-ordinal translation keys only exist for `workflowsPage.frequencies.ordinals.1` through `ordinals.10`. If translations for more (or fewer) written ordinals are ever added, this bound must be kept in sync manually, and the relationship between the literal and the translation data is not obvious to a reader.

Extract the bound into a named constant that documents the tie to the available translation keys, for example:

```tsx
// Written-ordinal translations exist only for 1-10; higher numbers fall back to the numeric ordinal.
const MAX_WRITTEN_ORDINAL = 10;

const toLocaleOrdinalWithWords: LocaleContextProps['toLocaleOrdinalWithWords'] = (number) => {
    if (number >= 1 && number <= MAX_WRITTEN_ORDINAL) {
        return translate(`workflowsPage.frequencies.ordinals.${number}` as TranslationPaths);
    }
    return toLocaleOrdinalLocaleDigitUtils(currentLocale, number);
};
```

The `2` in `Math.min(2, ...)` is a bare magic number controlling the maximum zoom-headroom factor. Unlike the neighboring `MAX_CANVAS_DIMENSION` (which is named), the reasoning for capping the headroom at exactly `2x` is not documented, so the intent isn't clear to a future reader.

Extract it into a named constant to match the `MAX_CANVAS_DIMENSION` pattern right beside it:

```tsx
const MAX_CANVAS_DIMENSION = 2048;
// Cap zoom headroom so the canvas is never drawn more than 2x larger than the fitted size.
const MAX_ZOOM_HEADROOM = 2;
const zoomHeadroom = Math.max(1, Math.min(MAX_ZOOM_HEADROOM, MAX_CANVAS_DIMENSION / Math.max(targetWidth, targetHeight, 1)));
```

`const headerExtraHeight = hasArchived ? 2 * 60 : 0;` uses undocumented magic numbers — `60` is presumably the pixel height of a section header and `2` is the number of headers rendered. Their meaning is not self-evident and they will silently drift if the header layout changes. Extract them into named constants.

```tsx
const SECTION_HEADER_HEIGHT = 60;
const SECTION_HEADER_COUNT = 2;
// ...
const headerExtraHeight = hasArchived ? SECTION_HEADER_COUNT * SECTION_HEADER_HEIGHT : 0;
```

`isPolicyArchived` returns `Math.random() < 0.5`, ignoring its `policy` argument entirely. The hardcoded `0.5` is a magic value, and more importantly this is placeholder/stub logic that will randomly classify workspaces as archived or active on every call — the WorkspaceSelector will bucket the same workspace into the "Active" or "Archived" section unpredictably across renders. Implement the real archived check off the policy's Onyx data instead of a random value.

```ts
function isPolicyArchived(policy: OnyxInputOrEntry<Policy>): boolean {
    // derive from the policy's report name value pairs / archived state, e.g.
    return getReportNameValuePairs(`${policy?.id}`)?.private_isArchived != null;
}
```

The hardcoded `2` in `if (result.length === 2)` is a magic number. Its intent — stop collecting once we know there is *more than one* eligible policy, because `getDefaultChatEnabledPolicy` only distinguishes between exactly one eligible policy (auto-select) and multiple (workspace selection) — is not self-evident from `=== 2`.

Extract it to a named constant that documents the intent:

```tsx
// We only need to know whether there is 0, exactly 1, or more than 1 eligible policy, so stop early once we exceed one.
const MAX_CHAT_ENABLED_POLICIES_TO_EVALUATE = 2;
// ...
if (result.length === MAX_CHAT_ENABLED_POLICIES_TO_EVALUATE) {
    break;
}
```

The new default parameter value `iconSize = 12` is a magic number. The codebase already defines this exact value as a named constant, `variables.iconSizeExtraSmall` (which equals `12`), and it is already used by other Search components (e.g. `DropdownButton.tsx`, `WithdrawalIDListItemHeader.tsx`). Using the raw literal here obscures its meaning and duplicates a value that lives in the style system.

Import `variables` and use the named constant as the default instead:

```tsx
import variables from '@styles/variables';

export default function TextWithIconCell({icon, iconSize = variables.iconSizeExtraSmall, text, showTooltip, textStyle, numberOfLines = 1}: TextWithIconCellProps) {
```

`COUNT_BAR_WIDTH + 16` uses an undocumented magic number `16` for the horizontal gap between the count and total skeleton bars, while every other layout dimension in this file is a named constant. This makes the intent unclear and harder to adjust.

Extract it to a named constant:

```tsx
const BAR_GAP = 16;
const TOTAL_BAR_OFFSET = COUNT_BAR_WIDTH + BAR_GAP;
```

The check `result?.jsonCode === 200` uses a hardcoded magic number for the HTTP/JSON success code. The codebase already defines a named constant for this exact value (`CONST.JSON_CODE.SUCCESS === 200`), which is used consistently elsewhere in this same file and across the app (e.g. `src/libs/actions/OnyxUpdates.ts`, `src/libs/Network/SequentialQueue.ts`). Using the raw `200` here reduces readability and diverges from the established convention.

Replace the literal with the named constant:

```ts
if (result?.jsonCode === CONST.JSON_CODE.SUCCESS && response?.data === undefined) {
    Log.info('[Search] loading_terminal_empty', false, {hash: queryJSON.hash, type: queryJSON.type});
}
```

The page size `100` is a magic number used in two coupled places inside `getDeployedCommitMessages`: `per_page: 100` and the loop-termination check `if (tags.length < 100)`. If one is changed without the other, pagination silently breaks. Extract it into a named constant so the coupling is explicit and self-documenting.

```ts
const TAGS_PER_PAGE = 100;
// ...
const {data: tags} = await GithubUtils.octokit.repos.listTags({
    owner: CONST.GITHUB_OWNER,
    repo: CONST.APP_REPO,
    per_page: TAGS_PER_PAGE,
    page,
});
// ...
if (tags.length < TAGS_PER_PAGE) {
    break;
}
```

The `256` in `{maxWidth: 256}` is an undocumented magic number. Its purpose (the fixed max width cap for the filter pill so the label truncates with an ellipsis) is only explained in the PR description, not in the code. A named constant makes the intent self-documenting and lets the cap be reused/tuned in one place.

Extract it into a named constant or a `variables`/`styles` entry, e.g.:

```tsx
// in @styles/variables
filterPillMaxWidth: 256,

// usage
innerStyles={[isExpanded && styles.buttonHoveredBG, {maxWidth: variables.filterPillMaxWidth}, styles.flexShrink1, innerStyles, shouldShowCloseButton && styles.pr2]}
```

The raw literal `200` is used for the HTTP success check. The codebase defines a named constant `CONST.JSON_CODE.SUCCESS` (= 200) that most response-handling code uses (e.g. `response?.jsonCode !== CONST.JSON_CODE.SUCCESS`), which makes the intent clearer than a bare number. Note there is precedent for the raw literal in sibling files (`OnyxUpdates.ts`, `OnyxUpdateManager/index.ts`), so this is a low-confidence suggestion.

```ts
import CONST from '@src/CONST';

// ...
if (!response?.onyxData || response.jsonCode !== CONST.JSON_CODE.SUCCESS) {
    return response;
}
```

The NitroFetch target discriminator `'fetch'` is hardcoded as a magic string. It appears here (`target: 'fetch'`) and in `clearTokenRefresh('fetch')` on line 45 of this file, as well as in `clearPrefetchOnAppStart/index.ts`. Repeating this bare literal across call sites is easy to typo and hard to trace. Note this file already extracts `CONTENT_TYPE_HEADER` and the PR adds `CONST.HTTP_HEADER_NAMES.AUTH_TOKEN`, so this value should be a named constant too.

Extract a named constant and reuse it:
```ts
const NITRO_FETCH_TARGET = 'fetch';
// ...
clearTokenRefresh(NITRO_FETCH_TARGET);
// ...
registerTokenRefresh({
    target: NITRO_FETCH_TARGET,
    // ...
});
```

The bare boolean literal `true` is passed positionally as the third argument to `getRoute(policyID, CONST.CERTINIA_PREREQUISITES.PAGE_NAME.INSTALL_BUNDLE, true)`. At the call site the meaning of `true` is not self-explanatory — a reader has to jump to the `getRoute` signature to learn it means `isSandbox`. This hurts readability and is easy to get wrong.

Introduce a named constant (or local variable) to make the intent explicit, e.g.:

```tsx
const IS_SANDBOX = true;
// ...
Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.POLICY_ACCOUNTING_CERTINIA_PREREQUISITES.getRoute(policyID, CONST.CERTINIA_PREREQUISITES.PAGE_NAME.INSTALL_BUNDLE, IS_SANDBOX));
```

This clarifies at the call site that the sandbox flow is being requested.

The ellipsis string `'...'` is hardcoded here, but the codebase already exports a named constant for exactly this value: `ELLIPSIS = '...'` in `@components/Charts/VictoryTheme`. Duplicating the literal makes the intent less clear and means a future change to the ellipsis glyph would have to be made in multiple places.

Import and use the existing constant instead:

```tsx
import VictoryTheme, {CHART_CONTENT_MIN_HEIGHT, ELLIPSIS, GLYPH_PADDING} from '@components/Charts/VictoryTheme';

// ...
ellipsisWidth={measureTextWidth(ELLIPSIS, fontManager, variables.iconSizeExtraSmall)}
```

The heap-pressure thresholds `80` and `50` are magic numbers. Unlike `getSessionDurationBucket`, whose boundaries are mirrored in its return labels (`'0-5'`, `'6-30'`, …), the `'high'`/`'mid'`/`'low'` labels here don't reveal what percentages they correspond to, so the meaning of `80` and `50` is opaque to a reader.

Extract them into named constants so the cutoffs are self-documenting:

```ts
const HEAP_PRESSURE_HIGH_PCT = 80;
const HEAP_PRESSURE_MID_PCT = 50;

function getHeapPressureBucket(record: SessionRecord): string {
    // ...
    const pct = (record.peakUsedJSHeapSizeMB / limitMB) * 100;
    if (pct >= HEAP_PRESSURE_HIGH_PCT) {
        return 'high';
    }
    if (pct >= HEAP_PRESSURE_MID_PCT) {
        return 'mid';
    }
    return 'low';
}
```

The `+ 9` in `tooltipTexts.slice(avatarRows.length * maxAvatarsInRow - 1, avatarRows.length * maxAvatarsInRow + 9)` is an undocumented magic number. The previous implementation (`ReportActionAvatar.tsx`) carried an explanatory comment ("We only want to cap tooltips to only 10 users or so since some reports have hundreds of users, causing performance to degrade."), which was dropped in this refactor, leaving the intent of `9` unclear.

Restore the explanatory comment or extract a named constant, e.g.:
```tsx
const MAX_TOOLTIP_USERS = 10;
// Cap tooltips to ~10 users; some reports have hundreds and degrade performance
text={tooltipTexts.slice(avatarRows.length * maxAvatarsInRow - 1, avatarRows.length * maxAvatarsInRow + (MAX_TOOLTIP_USERS - 1)).join(', ')}
```

The generated letter-avatar path string `'/images/avatars/generated/letter/'` is hardcoded here and the same path prefix is duplicated when building the URL in `getLetterAvatarURL` (`${CONST.CLOUDFRONT_URL}/images/avatars/generated/letter/v1/...`). A magic path literal repeated across functions is fragile — if the backend path changes, both spots must be updated in lockstep, and the detection check can silently drift from the construction.

Extract the shared path segment into a named constant (e.g. in `CONST`) and reference it in both `isGeneratedLetterAvatarURL` and `getLetterAvatarURL`:
```ts
// CONST
GENERATED_LETTER_AVATAR_PATH: '/images/avatars/generated/letter/',

// usage
return typeof avatarSource === 'string' && avatarSource.includes(CONST.GENERATED_LETTER_AVATAR_PATH);
```

`trimmedQuery.length <= 2` uses an undocumented magic number. The `2` encodes the minimum query length required before navigation suggestions are computed, but its meaning is not obvious from the call site.

Extract it into a named constant alongside `MAX_NAVIGATION_SUGGESTIONS`:

```tsx
const MIN_NAVIGATION_QUERY_LENGTH = 2;
// ...
if (trimmedQuery.length <= MIN_NAVIGATION_QUERY_LENGTH && !isNavigationIntentOnly) {
    return [];
}
```

The value `0.25` in `rowHeight * (1 - 0.25)` is an undocumented magic number representing the bar inner padding. The codebase already defines this concept as the named constant `BAR_INNER_PADDING` (`0.3`) in `@components/Charts/barChartConstants`, which the sibling `BarChartContent.tsx` and even the new `HorizontalBarSeries.tsx` use for the same calculation. Hardcoding `0.25` here is both a magic number and inconsistent with that constant (the hit-test box width will not match the rendered bar height).

Import and reuse the shared constant so the hit-test geometry stays in sync with the rendered bars:
```ts
import BAR_INNER_PADDING from '@components/Charts/barChartConstants';
// ...
barHeight.set(rowHeight * (1 - BAR_INNER_PADDING));
```

The hardcoded WASM URL embeds the `@lottiefiles/dotlottie-web` version (`@0.44.0`). This duplicates the version pinned in `package.json`/`package-lock.json`, so a future bump of `@lottiefiles/dotlottie-react` (which pulls a new `dotlottie-web`) will silently leave this URL pointing at the old WASM binary, causing a version mismatch that is easy to miss.

Extract the URL (or at least the version) to a named constant in `CONST.ts` and reference it here, so the version lives in one documented place:

```ts
// CONST.ts
DOTLOTTIE_WASM_URL: 'https://cdn.expensify.com/cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@lottiefiles/dotlottie-web@0.44.0/dist/dotlottie-player.wasm',

// App.tsx
setWasmUrl(CONST.DOTLOTTIE_WASM_URL);
```

The expression `1000 * 60 * 60 * 24` is a magic number representing milliseconds per day. The nearby values `-30` (line 87) and `14` (line 92) are also magic numbers representing day thresholds for trial status. These should be extracted to named constants for clarity.

Define named constants:
```ts
const MS_PER_DAY = 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24;
const EXPIRED_THRESHOLD_DAYS = -30;
const EXPIRING_SOON_THRESHOLD_DAYS = 14;
```
Then use them in `getDaysTillDate` and `getFreeTrialStatus`.

The `SpendRulePhraseNoun` type uses raw string literals `'merchant'` and `'spendCategory'`, but the adjacent `SpendRulePhraseAdjective` type follows the pattern of referencing `typeof CONST.SPEND_RULES.ACTION.BLOCK | typeof CONST.SPEND_RULES.ACTION.ALLOW`. For consistency and maintainability, the noun values should also be defined in `CONST.SPEND_RULES`.

Add a `NOUN` (or similar) section to `CONST.SPEND_RULES` and reference it in the type:

```ts
// In CONST/index.ts under SPEND_RULES:
NOUN: {
    MERCHANT: 'merchant',
    SPEND_CATEGORY: 'spendCategory',
},
```

```ts
// In SpendRuleChangeLogUtils.ts:
type SpendRulePhraseNoun = typeof CONST.SPEND_RULES.NOUN.MERCHANT | typeof CONST.SPEND_RULES.NOUN.SPEND_CATEGORY | '';
```

Then replace the string literals `'merchant'` (line 377) and `'spendCategory'` (line 386) with `CONST.SPEND_RULES.NOUN.MERCHANT` and `CONST.SPEND_RULES.NOUN.SPEND_CATEGORY`.

The value `40` represents `4 * PUSHER_DRAFT_PACE_INTERVAL_MS` (where the interval is 10ms), and `10` on line 224 represents `1 * PUSHER_DRAFT_PACE_INTERVAL_MS`. These magic numbers are directly derived from the pacing interval constant but do not reference it. If `PUSHER_DRAFT_PACE_INTERVAL_MS` changes, these tests will silently produce incorrect assertions.

Since the constant is not exported, consider either exporting it or defining a local mirror constant in the test file:

```ts
const PUSHER_DRAFT_PACE_INTERVAL_MS = 10;
// ...
now += 4 * PUSHER_DRAFT_PACE_INTERVAL_MS;
// ...
now += PUSHER_DRAFT_PACE_INTERVAL_MS;
```

The string `'submit2026'` is a magic value. The constant `CONST.POLICY.TYPE.SUBMIT` is already imported and used elsewhere in this file (in `makeSubmitPolicy`). Using the raw string in the mock makes it fragile if the constant value ever changes, and less readable than the named constant.

The same issue applies to the `isGroupPolicy` mock on the line that adds `|| policy?.type === 'submit2026'`.

Replace the magic string with the constant:
```tsx
// In getDefaultChatEnabledPolicy mock:
if (activePolicy && activePolicy.isPolicyExpenseChatEnabled && (activePolicy.type === 'team' || activePolicy.type === 'corporate' || activePolicy.type === CONST.POLICY.TYPE.SUBMIT)) {

// In isGroupPolicy mock:
isGroupPolicy: jest.fn((policy: OnyxEntry<Policy>) => policy?.type === 'team' || policy?.type === 'corporate' || policy?.type === CONST.POLICY.TYPE.SUBMIT),
```

Note: The pre-existing `'team'` and `'corporate'` strings have the same issue but are outside the scope of this diff.

The value `12` in `{bottom: 12}` is an unexplained magic number controlling the pagination-dot offset from the bottom of the illustration.

Extract it to a named constant or reuse an existing spacing variable:
```tsx
const PAGINATION_DOT_BOTTOM_OFFSET = 12;
// ...
style={[styles.pAbsolute, styles.flexRow, styles.justifyContentCenter, styles.w100, styles.l0, styles.r0, {bottom: PAGINATION_DOT_BOTTOM_OFFSET}]}
```

The string `'startDate,endDate'` is a magic composite value used to identify a multi-field update. Unlike the other `updatedField` comparisons in this function (e.g., `'name'`, `'rate'`, `'enabled'`) which are single self-explanatory field names, this comma-separated compound string is fragile and not self-documenting. If the backend ever changes the delimiter or ordering, this will silently break.

Consider extracting this to a named constant:
```ts
// In CONST or at module level
const UPDATED_FIELD_START_AND_END_DATE = 'startDate,endDate';

// Usage
if (customUnitRateName && updatedField === UPDATED_FIELD_START_AND_END_DATE && startDate && endDate) {
```

The magic number `13` passed to `generateHexadecimalValue(13)` lacks a named constant explaining what it represents (the length of a hexadecimal ID). This same magic number appears in multiple files across the codebase without a shared constant.

Extract it into a named constant, e.g.:
```ts
const HEX_ID_LENGTH = 13;
// ...
const rateID = existingTargetRateID ?? generateHexadecimalValue(HEX_ID_LENGTH);
```

Note: This is a pre-existing pattern in the file and codebase, so this may be intentionally deferred to a separate cleanup.

`{paddingBottom: 4}` is a magic number with no explanation. It should be extracted to a named style constant or use an existing theme style.

Replace the inline magic number with a named constant or a theme style, for example:
```tsx
// In styles
const GROUP_CHILDREN_PADDING_BOTTOM = 4;
// Or use an existing spacing value from the theme
```

The hardcoded string `` `search/view/${reportID}` `` is a magic value that duplicates the route path defined in `DYNAMIC_ROUTES.SEARCH_REPORT_VIEW`. If the route path changes in `ROUTES.ts`, this string will silently become stale.

Use the route definition instead. For example, since this is doing a substring match on the active route URL, you could derive the expected substring from `DYNAMIC_ROUTES.SEARCH_REPORT_VIEW.getRoute(reportID)` or construct it from `DYNAMIC_ROUTES.SEARCH_REPORT_VIEW.path`:
```ts
} else if (activeRoute.includes(DYNAMIC_ROUTES.SEARCH_REPORT_VIEW.getRoute(reportID))) {
```

`zIndex: 1000` is a magic number with no explanation of why this specific value was chosen. If a different component uses a different z-index, it will be hard to reason about layering.

Extract this to a named constant (e.g., in `CONST` or `variables`) to document its purpose:
```tsx
// In CONST or variables
const SAFE_TRIANGLE_Z_INDEX = 1000;

// In the component
style={[styles.pFixed, styles.cursorPointer, svgRect, {zIndex: SAFE_TRIANGLE_Z_INDEX}]}
```

The string `'(untagged)'` is a magic string used without being defined as a named constant. This same value appears in multiple places in the codebase and should be centralized in `CONST.SEARCH` (e.g., `CONST.SEARCH.TAG_UNTAGGED_VALUE`) for maintainability and consistency, similar to how `TAG_EMPTY_VALUE` is already defined there.

Suggested fix: Add a constant to `CONST.SEARCH` and reference it here:
```ts
// In CONST/index.ts under SEARCH:
TAG_UNTAGGED_VALUE: '(untagged)',

// In buildTagGroupQuery:
if (!tag || tag === CONST.SEARCH.TAG_EMPTY_VALUE || tag === CONST.SEARCH.TAG_UNTAGGED_VALUE) {
```

The value `60000` (milliseconds per minute) is used as a magic number here and again on line 45. Since it appears twice and is not immediately self-explanatory without the comment, it should be extracted into a named constant for clarity and consistency with the already-named `POLL_INTERVAL_MS`.

Suggested fix:
```ts
const MS_PER_MINUTE = 60_000;
```
Then replace both occurrences:
```ts
const currentMinute = Math.floor(now.getTime() / MS_PER_MINUTE);
// ...
lastMinute = Math.floor(snapshot.getTime() / MS_PER_MINUTE);
```

The magic number `8` in `variables.componentBorderRadiusRounded + 8` is not self-explanatory. While the comment above explains the general intent ("the overlap must be ≥ the composer's corner radius"), the `8` itself is unexplained — it is unclear what it represents or why this specific value was chosen.

Extract it into a named constant that documents its purpose:
```ts
const BANNER_EXTRA_OVERLAP_PX = 8;
const BANNER_COMPOSER_OVERLAP_PX = variables.componentBorderRadiusRounded + BANNER_EXTRA_OVERLAP_PX;
```

The number `15` in `variables.lineHeightNormal * 15` is a magic number without documentation. It is unclear what `15` represents (maximum visible lines? a design spec value?).

Extract it into a named constant to clarify its intent:
```tsx
/** Maximum number of visible lines before the prompt input stops auto-growing */
const MAX_VISIBLE_PROMPT_LINES = 15;

// Then in JSX:
maxAutoGrowHeight={variables.lineHeightNormal * MAX_VISIBLE_PROMPT_LINES}
```

The string `'__none_option__'` is a magic value duplicated from the `NONE_OPTION_KEY` constant defined in `SearchSingleSelectionPicker.tsx`. If the constant value changes, this test will silently break.

Export `NONE_OPTION_KEY` from `SearchSingleSelectionPicker.tsx` and import it in the test:

```tsx
import SearchSingleSelectionPicker, { NONE_OPTION_KEY } from '@components/Search/SearchSingleSelectionPicker';

// Then use it:
const noneItem = screen.getByTestId(`${CONST.BASE_LIST_ITEM_TEST_ID}${NONE_OPTION_KEY}`);
```

The fallback filename `'chat_attachment'` is a magic string. The same string appears in `src/components/AttachmentPicker/index.native.tsx`. Extract it to a shared constant (e.g., in `CONST.ts`) to avoid duplication and improve maintainability.

```ts
// In CONST.ts
DEFAULT_ATTACHMENT_FILENAME: 'chat_attachment',

// Usage
const durableUri = await moveReceiptToDurableStorage(file.uri, file.name ?? CONST.DEFAULT_ATTACHMENT_FILENAME);
```

The value `60000` (milliseconds per minute) is a magic number that reduces readability. While `60` for minutes-per-hour is relatively self-explanatory, `60000` requires the reader to recognize it as a unit conversion.

Extract this to a named constant:

```tsx
const MS_PER_MINUTE = 60000;

function formatElapsedTime(startTime: string): string {
    const elapsedMs = Date.now() - new Date(`${startTime}Z`).getTime();
    const totalMinutes = Math.floor(elapsedMs / MS_PER_MINUTE);
    const hours = Math.floor(totalMinutes / 60);
    const minutes = totalMinutes % 60;
